{{ define "help" }}
<h1 id="landlord">Landlord</h1>
<p>Landlord is an app that manages multi-tenancy in AWS. It is designed
to be used with AWS Cognito. Landlord supports tenancy by creating
custom claims and groups in AWS Cognito. Further, the Landlord webapp
console provides a means to manage tenants, users and groups.</p>
<h2 id="landlord-json-web-tokens">Landlord JSON Web Tokens</h2>
<p>JSON Web Tokens (<a href="https://jwt.io/">JWTs</a>) are the means of
communicating identity data when using AWS Cognito and Landlord. Claims
on a JWT are attributes that are signed and can be trusted by the
recipient of the JWT.</p>
<p>The AWS Cognito claims of interest are:</p>
<pre><code>* `cognito:groups`
* `email`
* `sub`</code></pre>
<p>Landlord adds custom claims to the JWTs issued by AWS Cognito to
support tenancy and impersonation:</p>
<pre><code>* `custom:tenant`
* `custom:user`
* `custom:impersonate_tenant`
* `custom:impersonate_group`
* `custom:impersonate_email`
* `custom:impersonate_sub`
* `custom:impersonate_user_id`</code></pre>
<h2 id="aws-cognito-claims">AWS Cognito Claims</h2>
<p>The <code>cognito:groups</code> claim is used to model the roles of a
user. For example, for the role “admin” in the tenant “example-tenant”,
where the tenant has the UUID:
<code>123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000</code>, the group claim is
named <code>tenant-123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000-admin</code>.
Landlord ensures that at any given point, a user has at most one role
and thus only one entry in the <code>cognito:groups</code> claim.</p>
<p>The <code>email</code> claim on the JWT is a unique identifier of the
user. The uniqueness of an email address across AWS User Pools within an
AWS account is not guaranteed. However, within a set of tenants managed
by Landlord, uniqueness of the email address is enforced.</p>
<p>The <code>sub</code> claim is a UUID generated by Cognito to uniquely
identify the user (a.k.a. the <em>subject</em>).</p>
<h2 id="landlord-claims">Landlord Claims</h2>
<p>Landlord adds two features to the JWTs issued by AWS Cognito: tenancy
and impersonation.</p>
<p>Tenancy is modeled by the <code>custom:tenant</code> claim. This
claim is a UUID that identifies the tenant. All JWTs issued in a
Landlord managed system will include this claim. There is always, at
least, a <code>root</code> tenant defined.</p>
<p>The <code>custom:user</code> claim is a UUID that identifies the
user. This claim is added to the JWT when a user is created. This ID
differs from the Cognito <code>sub</code> claim, which is a UUID
generated by Cognito to uniquely identify the user. Applications that
rely on Landlord JWTs should use the <code>custom:user</code> claim.
This allows for the user’s Cognito credentials to be easily
migrated.</p>
<p>Impersonation is a feature that allows a user to act as another user.
When impersonating, additional claims are added to the JWT:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>custom:impersonate_tenant</code>: The UUID of a tenant that is
currently being impersonated.</li>
<li><code>custom:impersonate_group</code>: The name of a group that is
currently being impersonated.</li>
<li><code>custom:impersonate_email</code> (optional): The
<code>email</code> claim of the user that is currently being
impersonated.</li>
<li><code>custom:impersonate_sub</code> (optional): The <code>sub</code>
claim of the user that is currently being impersonated.</li>
<li><code>custom:impersonate_user_id</code>: The
<code>custom:user</code> claim of the user that is currently being
impersonated.</li>
</ul>
<p>When impersonating, the <code>custom:impersonate_email</code>,
<code>custom:impersonate_user_id</code> and
<code>custom:impersonate_sub</code> claims are optional. If the
<code>custom:impersonate_group</code> claim is set, but the
<code>custom:impersonate_email</code>,
<code>custom:impersonate_user_id</code> and
<code>custom:impersonate_sub</code> claims are not set, the user
represented by the <code>sub</code>, <code>user</code>, and
<code>email</code> claims should be considered part of the impersonated
tenant and group.</p>
<p>A user has five parts: <code>tenant</code>, <code>group</code>,
<code>email</code>, <code>sub</code>, and <code>user</code>.</p>
<p>Note that when impersonating a user, whenever an application
identifies a user (e.g., in UI, messages or logs), it should identify
both the original user and the impersonated user.</p>
{{ end }}
